  <!-- Comprehensive Explanatory Section -->
  <section class="w-full py-16 bg-canvas border-t border-hairline mt-12">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 space-y-14">
      <div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <h3 class="text-[11px] font-bold text-mute uppercase tracking-widest whitespace-nowrap">Neuroscience & Methodology</h3>
          <div class="h-px w-full bg-hairline"></div>
        </div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink mb-4">The Science Behind the Pomodoro Technique</h2>
        <p class="text-body leading-relaxed text-base mb-4">
          Developed in the late 1980s by university student Francesco Cirillo, the Pomodoro Technique is a time-management methodology that transforms time from an adversary into an ally. Named after the Italian word for tomato—after the tomato-shaped kitchen timer Cirillo used—the core protocol alternates 25 minutes of single-task immersion with 5 minutes of deliberate cognitive rest.
        </p>
        <p class="text-body leading-relaxed text-base">
          Modern neuroscience and cognitive psychology validate this cadence. According to <strong>Attention Restoration Theory (ART)</strong> and research on working memory capacity, the human brain’s prefrontal cortex exhausts its executive attentional resources after 20 to 40 minutes of intense concentration. Brief pauses replenish glycogen stores, flush out cognitive fatigue, and mitigate the subconscious anxiety caused by <em>Parkinson’s Law</em>—the adage that work expands to fill the time allotted for its completion. For extended writing projects, you can also pair your focus blocks with our <a href="/word-counter/" class="text-link font-medium hover:underline">word counter tool</a> or explore our guide on <a href="/blog/10-proven-productivity-techniques/" class="text-link font-medium hover:underline">10 proven productivity techniques</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 sm:gap-8">
        <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 bg-canvas border border-hairline rounded-3xl shadow-sm hover:shadow-md hover:border-link/20 transition-all">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mb-2">Combating Cognitive Friction</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
            The hardest part of any demanding task is initiation. By committing to just one 25-minute block rather than an overwhelming 8-hour workday, you lower the brain's emotional resistance to starting. Once underway, the <em>Zeigarnik Effect</em> takes over, creating natural cognitive momentum to see the task through to completion.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 bg-canvas border border-hairline rounded-3xl shadow-sm hover:shadow-md hover:border-link/20 transition-all">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mb-2">The Importance of Disconnected Rest</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
            A 5-minute break is not an invitation to check social media, read emails, or process secondary messages. Screen-based stimuli keep the brain’s default mode network active and sustain mental exhaustion. True restoration involves standing up, looking out a window, hydrating, or gentle physical stretching.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <h3 class="text-[11px] font-bold text-mute uppercase tracking-widest whitespace-nowrap">Rhythm & Timeboxing</h3>
          <div class="h-px w-full bg-hairline"></div>
        </div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink mb-4">Focus Protocol Comparison: Finding Your Optimal Rhythm</h2>
        <p class="text-body leading-relaxed text-base mb-6">
          While the traditional 25/5 interval is the gold standard for initiating work, different cognitive tasks benefit from alternate timeboxing protocols:
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-hairline bg-canvas shadow-sm">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead class="bg-canvas-soft-2/80 border-b border-hairline text-ink font-semibold">
              <tr>
                <th class="p-4 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-mute">Focus Protocol</th>
                <th class="p-4 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-mute">Work / Rest Ratio</th>
                <th class="p-4 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-mute">Best Suited For</th>
                <th class="p-4 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-mute">Cognitive Benefit</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline text-body">
              <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft-2/40 transition-colors">
                <td class="p-4 font-semibold text-ink">Classic Pomodoro</td>
                <td class="p-4 font-medium text-ink">25m work / 5m rest</td>
                <td class="p-4">High-procrastination tasks, exam studying, administrative duties</td>
                <td class="p-4">Lowest initiation barrier; eliminates overwhelm</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft-2/40 transition-colors">
                <td class="p-4 font-semibold text-ink">The 52/17 Rule</td>
                <td class="p-4 font-medium text-ink">52m work / 17m rest</td>
                <td class="p-4">Knowledge workers, data analysis, deep writing</td>
                <td class="p-4">Statistically derived by DeskTime for peak productivity</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft-2/40 transition-colors">
                <td class="p-4 font-semibold text-ink">Ultradian Rhythm</td>
                <td class="p-4 font-medium text-ink">90m work / 20–30m rest</td>
                <td class="p-4">Software engineering, architectural design, creative writing</td>
                <td class="p-4">Matches human biological 90-minute alertness cycles</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft-2/40 transition-colors">
                <td class="p-4 font-semibold text-ink">The Flowtime Technique</td>
                <td class="p-4 font-medium text-ink">Variable (untimed) / 10–15m</td>
                <td class="p-4">Open-ended research, artistic flow states, prototyping</td>
                <td class="p-4">Prevents interrupting deep uninterrupted flow states</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <h3 class="text-[11px] font-bold text-mute uppercase tracking-widest whitespace-nowrap">Distraction Defense</h3>
          <div class="h-px w-full bg-hairline"></div>
        </div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink mb-4">Managing Distractions: The "Interruption Sheet" Strategy</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-body leading-relaxed text-base">
          <p>
            Distractions fall into two distinct categories: internal impulses and external interruptions. Managing both without derailing your timer is the secret to high focus scores:
          </p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6 pt-2">
            <div class="p-6 sm:p-7 bg-canvas border border-hairline rounded-3xl shadow-sm hover:shadow-md hover:border-link/20 transition-all">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-ink mb-2 text-base">Handling Internal Distractions</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
                When an unrelated thought strikes mid-session ("I need to pay that utility bill" or "Did I reply to that message?"), do not switch tabs. Instead, jot down a brief reminder on a physical notepad or in your task notes. Getting it out of working memory closes the mental loop, allowing you to resume your session immediately.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="p-6 sm:p-7 bg-canvas border border-hairline rounded-3xl shadow-sm hover:shadow-md hover:border-link/20 transition-all">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-ink mb-2 text-base">Protecting External Boundaries</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
                When colleagues or family members interrupt, apply Cirillo’s <em>Inform, Negotiate, Schedule, and Call Back</em> rule. Inform them that you are in the middle of a focused session, negotiate a quick follow-up window ("Can I come by your desk in 12 minutes?"), and return to your timer.
